# The oracle references, pinned.
#
# One line per reference, tab-separated: <name> <image> <version> <what it
# answers>.
#
# **Every line names the same image**, because a disagreement between two of
# these should be a fact about two parsers and never about two container
# builds. Three implementations that share no code for each protocol: llhttp
# (C), h11 (Python) and Go's net/http; nghttp2 (C), h2 (Python) and Go's
# x/net/http2; wsproto (Python), websockets (Python) and gorilla/websocket (Go).
# See containers/refs/Containerfile for why exactly these, and which others
# were counted and left out.
#
# The image is built here, so the tag is not a digest of the finished image:
# two builds of one Containerfile are not two copies of one image. What is
# pinned is everything the build reads - the base by digest, Debian's packages
# by full apt version against a dated snapshot, h11 by exact PyPI version - and
# the run-time version check is the real guarantee, so oracle_env.check_pin()
# is the thing that fails when an image drifts. There is no stock image named
# here and no host mode, so nothing relaxes that check.
#
# The version field is what the reference must say when asked. For llhttp it is
# llhttp's own version and not Node's (Node is only the carrier), though the
# probe prints both. For Go it is the toolchain that compiled net/http into the
# driver, because the HTTP code is Go's standard library at that release.
#
# Raising a pin is deliberate, and a separate commit from the change that makes
# the gate green: a new reference version is new answers, and a new answer that
# disagrees is a finding to triage, not a regression to fix.
#
# The tag is `deb13-N`: Debian 13 fixes the base, N moves whenever the image's
# contents can differ. deb13-2 added the three HTTP/2 references (nghttp2, h2,
# x/net/http2); deb13-3 added the three WebSocket ones (wsproto, websockets,
# gorilla/websocket). The earlier references are the same pins as in deb13-1
# and deb13-2.
#
# Building it:
#   make oracle-build

llhttp	localhost/ghoti-http-oracle-refs:deb13-3	llhttp 9.2.1	The parser inside Node, driven through its HTTPParser binding, lenient flags off
h11	localhost/ghoti-http-oracle-refs:deb13-3	h11 0.16.0	Sans-I/O HTTP/1.1 in Python; 0.16.0 is the release that closed the bare-LF chunk terminator hole
gohttp	localhost/ghoti-http-oracle-refs:deb13-3	go net/http go1.24.4	http.ReadRequest and http.ReadResponse and the body framing behind them
nghttp2	localhost/ghoti-http-oracle-refs:deb13-3	nghttp2 1.64.0	libnghttp2's server session, and its HPACK encoder and decoder, linked statically
h2	localhost/ghoti-http-oracle-refs:deb13-3	h2 4.4.1 (hpack 4.2.0, hyperframe 6.1.0)	Python sans-I/O HTTP/2 (h2) with its own HPACK (hpack) and framing (hyperframe) libraries
gox2	localhost/ghoti-http-oracle-refs:deb13-3	x/net/http2 v0.50.0 (go1.24.4)	Go's golang.org/x/net/http2 server, and its hpack encoder and decoder
wsproto	localhost/ghoti-http-oracle-refs:deb13-3	wsproto 1.3.2 (h11 0.16.0)	Python sans-I/O WebSocket (RFC 6455, permessage-deflate) on h11; the frame protocol and its extension, and the opening handshake
websockets	localhost/ghoti-http-oracle-refs:deb13-3	websockets 17.2	Python WebSocket's sans-I/O protocol layer (frames, handshake, permessage-deflate), with the I/O layer's message assembly and UTF-8 check done by the driver as the I/O layer does it
gows	localhost/ghoti-http-oracle-refs:deb13-3	gorilla/websocket v1.5.3 (go1.24.4)	Go's gorilla/websocket server and client, driven over a net.Conn of the driver's own; permessage-deflate without context takeover only
